    },
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "result": "CLEAR",
      "detail": "Angular derivative normalization is D_phys=L/(2*pi*i) d/dt. Disjoint-support factors 1/sqrt(2) and inverse pairing pi*i*m^2/(2L) are correct for a conjugate-linear first inner-product slot. The finite transfer retains q=1 Fourier-tail control and differentiated low-mode boundary/pole repairs; D norm at most K is applied only to finite corrections. The same-K inverse bound 2 transfers an approximant of the endpoint-normalized primitive to D^-1 v. Source/c-dependent constants precede L, and K=ceil L^4 absorbs polynomial losses into all-order rapid errors. This uses the prior reviewed DERIVED approximation chain, not a newly compiled theorem."
    },
    {
      "id": "F3_F4",
      "result": "CLEAR",
      "detail": "The repository paperFT has exp(+i*xi*t), so Plancherel has 1/(2*pi). Evenness doubles the half-line. Q>=1/2 on [0,1/s] gives ||H||^2>=s^-9/(144*pi)>2^-10*s^-9. The proved sinc decay gives |Q|^4<=16 exp(-sqrt(s*xi)); convexity bounds the fourth polynomial power by 8*(xi^8+1/256). The Gamma substitution contributes the required factor 2. Thus ||Hprime||^2<2^74*s^-11 and ||H||^3/||Hprime||>2^-52*s^-8=2^44, while m^2/2=2^-9. Hprime is nonzero since H is smooth, compactly supported and nonzero. No floating estimate or sign replacement of H is used."
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "result": "CLEAR",
      "detail": "For S>0, inverse Cauchy-Schwarz followed by Cauchy-Schwarz gives <u,S^-1u>>=||u||^3/||Su||. A unit-phase polar identification gives absolute inverse pairing equal to that positive quantity. The finite leading coefficients are respectively pi*m^2/(2L) and pi*||H||^3/(L*||Hprime||), with a strict positive difference. It follows that every sufficiently large L at the literal common cutoff excludes every unit-phase identification of these particular inputs. No ground, noncollision, off-line zero or RH hypothesis is introduced."
    },
    {
      "id": "F6",
      "result": "CLEAR",
      "detail": "For e_zeta=U* v-zeta u, the exact pairing with S^-1u and reverse triangle inequality give the claimed norm estimate. For large L its numerator is at least pi*Delta_H/(2L) and ||S^-1u||<=2||H||, yielding pi*Delta_H/(4||H||L). The whole unit-phase circle is handled uniformly. The lower bound decays like 1/L and concerns INPUT mismatch only."
    },
